Title: THE REUNION TABLE
Author: Carmen Delgado
Draft date: June 2026

FADE IN:


INT. MORALES HOME - KITCHEN - DAY

Preparation chaos. MARIA MORALES, 60s, directs grandchildren with oven mitts. Her son ANTONIO, 40s, chops onions in silence.
MARIA
Twenty people at six. Nobody fights at my table tonight.

INT. MORALES HOME - LIVING ROOM - DAY

Antonio's sister DIANA, 38, hangs old photos. One frame turned face-down — their father, absent twenty years.
DIANA
Mom still sets a plate for a ghost.
ANTONIO
Then let the ghost stay in the closet with the picture.

INT. MORALES HOME - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Full table. Laughter brittle. The doorbell rings. Maria freezes. On the porch: RAUL MORALES, 65, thin envelope in hand.
MARIA
You don't get to walk in like the years were a nap.

INT. MORALES HOME - DINING ROOM - NIGHT

Raul sits at the empty place. Antonio pushes back his chair. Grandchildren watch, sensing the temperature drop.
RAUL
I didn't come for forgiveness. I came to tell you what the lawyers kept.

INT. MORALES HOME - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Diana opens the envelope — deed papers, a key. Maria reads, hands shaking not with anger but disbelief.
MARIA
He left the house to all of us. Not to me alone.
ANTONIO
So he buys his way back with brick and mortar?
DIANA
No. He finally put our names on the same page.

FADE OUT.


THE END
